        "Internal assessments are set and marked inside the school, then externally moderated by the IB, and their weight varies by subject — roughly a fifth to a third of the final grade in most sciences and mathematics, more in language, arts and performance subjects. For Malaysian students this structure rewards process over a single test-day performance: a biology student needs a genuinely testable research question, a history student needs sources they can actually access, and an economics student needs current news articles suitable for the required commentaries.",
        "The Extended Essay is a 4,000-word independent research paper supervised by a school teacher through scheduled reflection meetings; TOK carries an exhibition plus a 1,600-word essay on a prescribed title. Both reward early, unglamorous choices — a question narrow enough to actually answer with sources available in Malaysia, and a reading list built before the school year's second term rather than scrambled together the week before a deadline.",
        "The line on academic honesty is firm and worth stating plainly to any tutor before booking them. Every IA, Extended Essay and TOK submission must be the student's own work, confirmed as such and checked through similarity detection. IB Gram tutors coach — they explain a misread criterion, model a parallel example that is not the assessed task, and question weak reasoning — but they do not write, rewrite line by line, or hand over text a student then submits under their own name.",
